#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105489 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a87f2499-6998-587a-930e-65845c94710c" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Intervals on Which the Derivative of a Graphed Function Is Positive or Negative"}] :question/id #uuid "6b6ba1b8-5041-57c3-bcbb-239af0faa4dd" :question/math-academy-id "e-3722" :question/problem "The graph of the continuous function $y=f(x)$ is shown below. The points $A$ and $B$ lie on the curve and have $x$-coordinates $x=1$ and $x=4.$ Given that $f'(x) = 0$ at both $A$ and $B,$ find the intervals where $f'(x)>0.$ \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14014428/assets/1a8208a75b7df62a0c692e08f74509ecf1462e3ab1f8dd7eae85a56272abde48.png)" :question/worked-solution "We have $f'(x)=0$ at the critical points $A$ and $B.$ Note the following:\n\n- If $f(x)$ is strictly increasing, then $f'(x)>0.$\n\n- If $f(x)$ is strictly decreasing, then $f'(x)<0.$\n\nThe information about $f'(x)$ can be summarized in the following table:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & (-\\infty ,1) & 1 & (1,4) & 4 & (4,+\\infty ) \\\\ f & ↘ & \\text{min} & ↗ & \\text{max} & ↘ \\\\ {f}^{′} & - & 0 & + & 0 & -\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f'(x)>0$ on the interval $(1,4).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105491 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3b466b35-cb4d-5082-9989-9c767af757f8" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Intervals on Which a Function Is Decreasing Using Differentiation"}] :question/id #uuid "7be416a1-e729-58a4-bc3c-d622d058eba8" :question/math-academy-id "e-3728" :question/problem "Find the values of $x$ for which the function $f(x) = x^3 + x^2 - 8x$ is strictly decreasing." :question/worked-solution "To find where $f(x)$ is strictly decreasing, we need to solve the inequality $f'(x) < 0.$\n\nDifferentiating $f(x),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =3{x}^{2}+2x-8,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso we need to solve the inequality\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{x}^{2}+2x-8<0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe above inequality factors as\n\n$$\n3(x + 2)\\left(x -\\dfrac 4 3\\right) < 0,\n$$\n\nand the graph of $y=3(x+2)\\left(x -\\dfrac 4 3\\right)$ has roots at $x=-2$ and $x=\\dfrac 4 3,$ as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14014428/assets/14e4f6078b21d209082c548f7f5d064abeaa98ee737aff919d2f6efad7a317e8.png)\n\nThe graph is negative on the interval $\\left( -2, \\dfrac{4}{3} \\right)$ between the roots, so we conclude that $f'(x) < 0$ on the interval $\\left( -2, \\dfrac{4}{3} \\right).$\n\nTherefore, $f(x)$ is strictly decreasing on the interval $\\left( -2, \\dfrac{4}{3} \\right).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105494 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1671fb4f-937f-5a3c-85b7-7f54f35ea543" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Intervals on Which a Function Is Increasing Using Differentiation"}] :question/id #uuid "cc1f0cb5-5cca-5a70-a89f-2983a64e273f" :question/math-academy-id "e-3731" :question/problem "Find the values of $x$ for which the function $f(x)=\\dfrac{\\ln{2x}}{x}$ (plotted below) is strictly increasing.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14014428/assets/2ccb7c95d06c78a1730f9ca46aad9b6f1370050c745f2c1fc9208275670c3649.png)" :question/worked-solution "To find where $f(x)$ is strictly increasing, we need to solve the inequality $f'(x) > 0.$\n\nDifferentiating $f(x)$ using the quotient rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{(\\frac{1}{x})\\cdot x-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x)\\cdot 1}{{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1-\\operatorname{ln}⁡2x}{{x}^{2}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nso we need to solve the inequality\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1-\\ln{2x}}{x^2} > 0.\n$$\n\nThe denominator is  always positive, so this reduces to\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1-\\operatorname{ln}⁡2x>0\\,⟹\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡2x<1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving $\\ln{2x} = 1$ gives the critical point\n\n$$\n2x = e\\quad \\Longrightarrow\\quad x = \\dfrac{1}{2}e.\n$$\n\nFrom the graph, we see that this critical point is a maximum. We also see that the slope of the tangent lines is positive to the left of the critical point and negative to the right of the critical point. \n\nWe summarize this information in the following table:\n\n$$\n\\left.\\begin{aligned}x & (0,\\frac{1}{2}e) & \\frac{1}{2}e & (\\frac{1}{2}e,\\infty ) \\\\ f & ↗ & \\text{max} & ↘ \\\\ {f}^{′} & + & 0 & -\\end{aligned}\\right|\n$$\n\nFinally, we conclude that the function is strictly increasing on the interval $\\left(0,\\frac 1 2 e\\right),$ which can also be written as $0 < x < \\dfrac 1 2 e.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105495 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b77217ef-9076-5365-bebb-d2b46e64c9be" :knowledge-point/title "Comparing Slopes of Functions at Different Points"}] :question/id #uuid "88cdcba5-2572-5a6a-aa99-826b80cb1d46" :question/math-academy-id "e-3734" :question/problem "Consider the graph of $y=f(x)$ below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14014428/assets/aa8ee7171576bc92bb9b6a6730223e2629bc0d6216cb54a7a8a9f51d97985d14.png)\n\nWhich of the following statements is true?\n\n- $f'(2.5) < f'(1) < f'(-1)$\n\n- $f'(-1) < f'(1) < f'(2.5)$\n\n- $f'(1) < f'(-1) < f'(2.5)$" :question/worked-solution "From the graph, we see that\n\n- the slope of the tangent at $x=-1$ is positive, so $f'(-1) > 0.$\n\n- the slope of the tangent at $x=1$ is zero, so $f'(1) = 0.$\n\n- the slope of the tangent at $x=2.5$ is negative, and so $f'(2.5) < 0.$\n\nTherefore, only statement I is correct."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186292228 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a87f2499-6998-587a-930e-65845c94710c" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Intervals on Which the Derivative of a Graphed Function Is Positive or Negative"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8cbe3b6e-00c7-5f39-897b-c2d8d8f84b73" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-\\infty ,4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292230} {:answer/id #uuid "ea0a253b-1b16-584f-a14a-5f7cba09daac" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-\\infty ,-2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292231} {:answer/id #uuid "fa52f26a-e08e-5419-9116-4d10f5295a3c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(-\\infty ,-4)$ and $(0,4)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292232} {:answer/id #uuid "96732919-bcea-5a5f-9bc1-c460d17cfa59" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(-\\infty ,-2)$ and $(-2,4)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292233} {:answer/id #uuid "b6f44e20-e37a-5efc-b8ed-53c6994d44e2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-2,4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292234}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "96732919-bcea-5a5f-9bc1-c460d17cfa59" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(-\\infty ,-2)$ and $(-2,4)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292233} :answer-field/id #uuid "405bddd1-c1a3-5ce7-a5b7-4815909f69c7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292229}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "09487e7d-c48e-5fae-b1d5-295ed803bdee" :question/math-academy-id "q-132596" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14014428/assets/d51ed648be79858e9dd211494f706c1f8658364e10d14dca8418b69cf4ab99de.png)\n\nThe graph of the continuous function $y=f(x)$ is shown above. The points $A$ and $B$ lie on the curve and have $x$-coordinates $x=-2$ and $x=4.$ Given that ${f}^{′}(x)=0$ at both $A$ and $B,$ find the intervals where ${f}^{′}(x)>0.$" :question/worked-solution "We have ${f}^{′}(x)=0$ at the critical points $A$ and $B.$ Note the following:\n\n- If $f(x)$ is strictly increasing, then ${f}^{′}(x)>0.$\n\n- If $f(x)$ is strictly decreasing, then ${f}^{′}(x)<0.$\n\nThe information about ${f}^{′}(x)$ can be summarized in the following table:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & (-\\infty ,-2) & -2 & (-2,4) & 4 & (4,\\infty ) \\\\ f & ↗ & \\text{no extr.} & ↗ & \\text{max} & ↘ \\\\ {f}^{′} & + & 0 & + & 0 & -\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${f}^{′}(x)>0$ on the intervals $(-\\infty ,-2)$ and $(-2,4).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186292235 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a87f2499-6998-587a-930e-65845c94710c" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Intervals on Which the Derivative of a Graphed Function Is Positive or Negative"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c84383f3-c389-5d03-8707-e04869d60dac" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292237} {:answer/id #uuid "b689ba88-6e6a-5b2b-b3bb-844dfcaa8814" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(-3,0)$ and $(3,\\infty )$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292238} {:answer/id #uuid "6b232530-216f-52de-856b-08518cf353c4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(-3,2)$ and $(2,\\infty )$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292239} {:answer/id #uuid "83e90022-91fd-5a4f-992b-56381c83960e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(-3,0)$ and $(4,\\infty )$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292240} {:answer/id #uuid "77c6da06-1c35-57c1-b3ee-1433d015a365" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(-3,-1)$ and $(2,\\infty )$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292241}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6b232530-216f-52de-856b-08518cf353c4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(-3,2)$ and $(2,\\infty )$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292239} :answer-field/id #uuid "a769bff4-4785-5bcc-8f36-894ecbe6504a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292236}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "245b4ca2-84ec-5d00-8249-a3c3d2070620" :question/math-academy-id "q-132598" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14014428/assets/3f542d08fede8a5bad978b62bece12070b02e0d3b4369c804454356f07d0832a.png)\n\nThe graph of the continuous function $y=f(x)$ is shown above. The points $A$ and $B$ lie on the curve and have $x$-coordinates $x=-3$ and $x=2.$ Given that ${f}^{′}(x)=0$ at both $A$ and $B,$ find the intervals where ${f}^{′}(x)>0.$" :question/worked-solution "We have ${f}^{′}(x)=0$ at the critical points $A$ and $B.$ Note the following:\n\n- If $f(x)$ is strictly increasing, then ${f}^{′}(x)>0.$\n\n- If $f(x)$ is strictly decreasing, then ${f}^{′}(x)<0.$\n\nThe information about ${f}^{′}(x)$ can be summarized in the following table:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & (-\\infty ,-3) & -3 & (-3,2) & 2 & (2,\\infty ) \\\\ f & ↘ & \\text{min} & ↗ & \\text{no extr.} & ↗ \\\\ {f}^{′} & - & 0 & + & 0 & +\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${f}^{′}(x)>0$ on the intervals $(-3,2)$ and $(2,\\infty ).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186292242 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a87f2499-6998-587a-930e-65845c94710c" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Intervals on Which the Derivative of a Graphed Function Is Positive or Negative"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "36425b4d-aecc-59dd-afcd-476a5ecb7910" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(-\\infty ,-3)$ and $(3,\\infty )$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292244} {:answer/id #uuid "cd287f3a-7a86-5f72-9d82-4c36657d016e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(-\\infty ,-3)$ and $(0,\\infty )$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292245} {:answer/id #uuid "183b364e-2f6e-5438-bde4-c1ddb2797290" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(-\\infty ,-3)$ and $(-3,0)$ and $(3,\\infty )$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292246} {:answer/id #uuid "095e3ea1-031c-54a8-bd35-01f7eab61423" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-\\infty ,-3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292247} {:answer/id #uuid "5a08669b-29c3-5531-afbc-116e314974ee" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(-\\infty ,0)$ and $(3,\\infty )$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292248}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "36425b4d-aecc-59dd-afcd-476a5ecb7910" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(-\\infty ,-3)$ and $(3,\\infty )$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292244} :answer-field/id #uuid "c9777dc0-ae21-5b75-a52d-70f9da361e59" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292243}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c7835afc-53a9-5dca-8971-8371ba5bec21" :question/math-academy-id "q-132605" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14014428/assets/799fd5d6528a9a66060d47e8f7613643ee5a305ed0dc494fabc1fc0ecf089e84.png)\n\nThe graph of the continuous function $y=f(x)$ is shown above. The points $A,B,$ and $C$ lie on the curve and have $x$-coordinates $x=-3,$ $x=0,$ and $x=3.$ Given that ${f}^{′}(x)=0$ at $A,B,$ and $C,$ find the intervals where ${f}^{′}(x)>0.$" :question/worked-solution "We have ${f}^{′}(x)=0$ at the critical points $A$ and $B.$ Note the following:\n\n- If $f(x)$ is strictly increasing, then ${f}^{′}(x)>0.$\n\n- If $f(x)$ is strictly decreasing, then ${f}^{′}(x)<0.$\n\nThe information about ${f}^{′}(x)$ can be summarized in the following table:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & (-\\infty ,-3) & -3 & (-3,0) & 0 & (0,3) & 3 & (3,\\infty ) \\\\ f & ↗ & \\text{max} & ↘ & \\text{no extr.} & ↘ & \\text{min} & ↗ \\\\ {f}^{′} & + & 0 & - & 0 & - & 0 & +\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${f}^{′}(x)>0$ on the intervals $(-\\infty ,-3)$ and $(3,\\infty ).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186292249 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3b466b35-cb4d-5082-9989-9c767af757f8" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Intervals on Which a Function Is Decreasing Using Differentiation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8d4a439c-3760-59ae-a952-2d06efece4ba" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in (-\\infty ,3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292251} {:answer/id #uuid "912e14a8-a29a-50a7-a750-2601c5237d35" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in (-3,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292252} {:answer/id #uuid "44fb95f6-be59-582b-abad-fcad5129bb73" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in (-\\infty ,\\frac{3}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292253} {:answer/id #uuid "45ab908e-f7fe-5596-9f80-61b384e27ab1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in (\\frac{3}{2},\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292254} {:answer/id #uuid "f57ab006-d7ec-56bd-b0c9-e86f260b841e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in (-\\infty ,-3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292255}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "44fb95f6-be59-582b-abad-fcad5129bb73" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in (-\\infty ,\\frac{3}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292253} :answer-field/id #uuid "9e22d4c4-0ef8-5d39-bcc4-6bfcf42e6175" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292250}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e994daf9-6dc5-55a7-aade-020d8278b5a2" :question/math-academy-id "q-132666" :question/problem "Find the values of $x$ for which the function $f(x)={e}^{2{x}^{2}-6x+3}$ is strictly decreasing." :question/worked-solution "The function is strictly decreasing where ${f}^{′}(x)<0.$ Differentiating $f(x),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x)={e}^{2{x}^{2}-6x+3}(4x-6),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso we need to solve the inequality\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{2{x}^{2}-6x+3}(4x-6)<0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe exponential function is always positive, so this reduces to\n\n$$\n4x-6<0.\n$$\n\nSolving this inequality, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4x-6 & <0 \\\\ 4x & <6 \\\\ x & <\\frac{3}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the function is strictly decreasing on the interval $(-\\infty ,\\frac{3}{2}).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186292256 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3b466b35-cb4d-5082-9989-9c767af757f8" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Intervals on Which a Function Is Decreasing Using Differentiation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "868bb96e-dd07-5c47-825c-10acbb5ebe98" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in (-\\infty ,1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292258} {:answer/id #uuid "12e3a044-d771-5cad-86ac-16428ca73b88" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in (\\sqrt{2},\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292259} {:answer/id #uuid "c110c872-9a41-53ea-b878-076c07c00a7e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in (-\\infty ,-\\sqrt{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292260} {:answer/id #uuid "1a25a8eb-1145-5fc1-bb38-4d7deea15be6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in (-\\sqrt{2},\\sqrt{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292261} {:answer/id #uuid "a9d214a8-df34-524d-97ad-a0c59561f163" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in (-\\infty ,0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292262}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a9d214a8-df34-524d-97ad-a0c59561f163" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in (-\\infty ,0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292262} :answer-field/id #uuid "ec6af44c-714f-596f-a227-c145e9a51b58" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292257}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "0ca3eeff-b7f5-5d53-aae2-987010ec68f9" :question/math-academy-id "q-132667" :question/problem "Find the values of $x$ for which the function $f(x)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2{x}^{2}+1)$ is strictly decreasing." :question/worked-solution "The function is strictly decreasing where ${f}^{′}(x)<0.$ Differentiating $f(x),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{1}{2{x}^{2}+1}\\cdot 4x \\\\  & =\\frac{4x}{2{x}^{2}+1},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso we need to solve the inequality\n\n$$\n\\frac{4x}{2{x}^{2}+1}<0.\n$$\n\nThe function $2{x}^{2}+1$ is always positive. So, this reduces to\n\n$$\n4x<0.\n$$\n\nSolving this inequality, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4x & <0 \\\\ x & <0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(x)$ is strictly decreasing on the interval $(-\\infty ,0).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186292263 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3b466b35-cb4d-5082-9989-9c767af757f8" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Intervals on Which a Function Is Decreasing Using Differentiation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d702f595-9b80-505e-a0e8-11068ce41807" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in (-5,5)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292265} {:answer/id #uuid "68555aac-b89a-510d-a9ce-03044e4b2d8e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in (\\frac{1}{2},\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292266} {:answer/id #uuid "2f7945b1-4f13-5c8f-b612-06ec4cbb2668" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in (-\\infty ,5)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292267} {:answer/id #uuid "b18e3d48-151c-55d3-ac35-0978af257cc3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in (-\\frac{1}{2},\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292268} {:answer/id #uuid "5221a555-a071-55a0-a13b-399329f6dbe3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in (-\\frac{1}{2},5)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292269}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5221a555-a071-55a0-a13b-399329f6dbe3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in (-\\frac{1}{2},5)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292269} :answer-field/id #uuid "9ddc9449-9d74-5e0f-8678-fd1854f2a545" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292264}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "4c459238-1e51-554b-a2be-46ee9a5e625e" :question/math-academy-id "q-132671" :question/problem "Find the values of $x$ for which the function $f(x)=4{x}^{3}-27{x}^{2}-30x+9$ is strictly decreasing." :question/worked-solution "The function is strictly decreasing where ${f}^{′}(x)<0.$ Differentiating $f(x),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =12{x}^{2}-54x-30 \\\\  & =6(2{x}^{2}-9x-5),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso we need to solve the inequality\n\n$$\n6(2{x}^{2}-9x-5)<0.\n$$\n\nThe above inequality factors as\n\n$$\n6(2x+1)(x-5)<0,\n$$\n\nand the graph of $y=6(2x+1)(x-5)$ has roots at $x=-\\frac{1}{2}$ and $x=5,$ as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14014428/assets/7eec3fc84b851b3b39e5ae8ecff1343f39393d1dd8f7a6cce8e8904dc1c65d48.png)\n\nThe graph is negative on the interval $(-\\frac{1}{2},5)$ between the roots, so we conclude that ${f}^{′}(x)<0$ on the interval $(-\\frac{1}{2},5).$\n\nTherefore, $f(x)$ is strictly decreasing on the interval $(-\\frac{1}{2},5).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186292270 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b77217ef-9076-5365-bebb-d2b46e64c9be" :knowledge-point/title "Comparing Slopes of Functions at Different Points"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fc400f9a-c327-5c5d-9b42-07292cf27567" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}^{′}(-2)<{f}^{′}(2)<{f}^{′}(5)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292272} {:answer/id #uuid "7fdcefba-ebda-5f40-83e8-eadf6611a610" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}^{′}(-2)<{f}^{′}(5)<{f}^{′}(2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292273} {:answer/id #uuid "908ecf58-8e2d-5ae6-aa67-e48528da0099" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}^{′}(2)<{f}^{′}(5)<{f}^{′}(-2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292274} {:answer/id #uuid "a616860f-5b4f-537c-9937-e4e42187c2e7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}^{′}(5)<{f}^{′}(-2)<{f}^{′}(2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292275} {:answer/id #uuid "926ed476-be9d-5b7f-9928-2d44068e3335" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}^{′}(5)<{f}^{′}(2)<{f}^{′}(-2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292276}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a616860f-5b4f-537c-9937-e4e42187c2e7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}^{′}(5)<{f}^{′}(-2)<{f}^{′}(2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292275} :answer-field/id #uuid "7e8314a6-a514-5ecf-8764-8e1dc9e2376c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292271}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "8c1ec922-2c78-50e0-8b35-5546601c3da9" :question/math-academy-id "q-132682" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14014428/assets/0ba11dcbffa05bb78ee8d9bdd6ca8618c789cf13ab208f17d169a7f700cf9e3e.png)\n\nConsider the graph of $y=f(x)$ above. Which of the following statements is true?" :question/worked-solution "From the graph, we see that\n\n- The slope of the tangent at $x=-2$ is zero, so ${f}^{′}(-2)=0.$\n\n- The slope of the tangent at $x=2$ is positive, so ${f}^{′}(2)>0.$\n\n- The slope of the tangent at $x=5$ is negative, so ${f}^{′}(5)<0.$\n\nTherefore, ${f}^{′}(5)<{f}^{′}(-2)<{f}^{′}(2).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186292277 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b77217ef-9076-5365-bebb-d2b46e64c9be" :knowledge-point/title "Comparing Slopes of Functions at Different Points"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a102c181-6fcb-5ed1-ac32-cf6ad8b7b882" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}^{′}(-1)<{f}^{′}(2)<{f}^{′}(0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292279} {:answer/id #uuid "9c205a26-5cd0-5dc1-9378-ea2978135209" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}^{′}(2)<{f}^{′}(-1)<{f}^{′}(0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292280} {:answer/id #uuid "762625ab-a2b2-5883-938b-0214796e9b8e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}^{′}(0)<{f}^{′}(2)<{f}^{′}(-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292281} {:answer/id #uuid "e4849d03-a641-5273-bab2-294f5e153518" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}^{′}(0)<{f}^{′}(-1)<{f}^{′}(2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292282} {:answer/id #uuid "1ba636d4-3511-5eaf-9ca0-daaedf356bb2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}^{′}(-1)<{f}^{′}(0)<{f}^{′}(2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292283}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1ba636d4-3511-5eaf-9ca0-daaedf356bb2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}^{′}(-1)<{f}^{′}(0)<{f}^{′}(2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292283} :answer-field/id #uuid "9659bd41-1c4c-58bd-8b01-5f609957e2d6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292278}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "1b02c1a1-391e-5d87-8195-8cddea5a07f7" :question/math-academy-id "q-132691" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14014428/assets/e0bd848039c3f2db6dd298af47eb907035c47c0c22c1cc72afaaa233f29a0195.png)\n\nConsider the graph of $y=f(x)$ above. Which of the following statements is true?" :question/worked-solution "From the graph, we see that\n\n- The slope of the tangent at $x=-1$ is negative, so ${f}^{′}(-1)<0.$\n\n- The slope of the tangent at $x=0$ is zero, so ${f}^{′}(0)=0.$\n\n- The slope of the tangent at $x=2$ is positive, so ${f}^{′}(2)>0.$\n\nTherefore, ${f}^{′}(-1)<{f}^{′}(0)<{f}^{′}(2).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106015 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a87f2499-6998-587a-930e-65845c94710c" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Intervals on Which the Derivative of a Graphed Function Is Positive or Negative"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a4256cc9-0a07-5c54-99b9-1a798c93de20" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(2, \\infty)$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186102328} {:answer/id #uuid "d52c0939-753a-541e-8daf-f26283cc2bdb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(- \\infty, 0)$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186102329} {:answer/id #uuid "dc545ee8-7ae9-5f37-806d-c46d2033a4e9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "No interval exists where $f^{′}(x) < 0$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186102330} {:answer/id #uuid "6064469b-87d0-50bf-a062-12a4fd32c094" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(- \\infty, 0)$ and $(2, \\infty)$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186102331} {:answer/id #uuid "716b2fa2-87c6-51ae-8fc6-b92fdf71ca00" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0, 2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186102332}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "716b2fa2-87c6-51ae-8fc6-b92fdf71ca00" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0, 2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186102332} :answer-field/id #uuid "1454d63e-c5a6-52c5-81d9-32e1a6416d94" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186102327}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "4c0a1416-8923-507f-b1c1-0e9a65b61f26" :question/math-academy-id "q-18227" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14014428/assets/269b71c2ec5653b6577e904a43374a45a0830f671850c7157a8d1bbfb49308f3.png)\n\nThe graph of the continuous function $y=f(x)$ is shown below. The points $A$ and $B$ lie on the curve and have $x$-coordinates $x=0$ and $x=2.$ Given that ${f}^{′}(x)=0$ at both $A$ and $B,$ find the intervals where ${f}^{′}(x)<0.$" :question/worked-solution "We have ${f}^{′}(x)=0$ at the critical points $A$ and $B.$ Note the following:\n\n- If $f(x)$ is strictly increasing, then ${f}^{′}(x)>0.$\n\n- If $f(x)$ is strictly decreasing, then ${f}^{′}(x)<0.$\n\nThe information about ${f}^{′}(x)$ can be summarized in the following table:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & (-\\infty ,0) & 0 & (0,2) & 2 & (2,\\infty ) \\\\ f & ↗ & \\text{max} & ↘ & \\text{min} & ↗ \\\\ {f}^{′} & + & 0 & - & 0 & +\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${f}^{′}(x)<0$ on the interval $(0,2).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186292284 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3b466b35-cb4d-5082-9989-9c767af757f8" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Intervals on Which a Function Is Decreasing Using Differentiation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d0b0d7b2-3ccc-5e5f-a751-92a8b183ffe4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in (\\frac{7}{8},\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292286} {:answer/id #uuid "3a7873af-440e-5a68-9a67-c3da6cd2c3bb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in (0,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292287} {:answer/id #uuid "e8418ace-cb19-54e4-be87-cd06a1a42793" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in (-\\frac{7}{8},\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292288} {:answer/id #uuid "cde47b04-4471-546f-b90b-d38396d71a07" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in (-\\infty ,\\frac{7}{8})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292289} {:answer/id #uuid "2b774eca-f0c2-58a1-af0f-cc7330fd2fca" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in (-\\infty ,-\\frac{7}{8})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292290}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e8418ace-cb19-54e4-be87-cd06a1a42793" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in (-\\frac{7}{8},\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292288} :answer-field/id #uuid "3563b7f6-7726-5e63-bb4d-9274479e90e9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292285}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ebb3d0c2-683e-5c49-bf5b-f1008fff0100" :question/math-academy-id "q-21017" :question/problem "Find the values of $x$ for which the function $f(x)=5-7x-4{x}^{2}$ is strictly decreasing." :question/worked-solution "The function is strictly decreasing where ${f}^{′}(x)<0.$ Differentiating $f(x),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =-7-8x \\\\  & =-8(x+\\frac{7}{8}),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso we need to solve the inequality\n\n$$\n-8(x+\\frac{7}{8})<0.\n$$\n\nSolving this inequality, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-8(x+\\frac{7}{8}) & <0 \\\\ 0 & <8(x+\\frac{7}{8}) \\\\ 0 & <x+\\frac{7}{8} \\\\ -\\frac{7}{8} & <x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe conclude that $f(x)$ is strictly decreasing on the interval $x\\in (-\\frac{7}{8},\\infty ).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186292291 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1671fb4f-937f-5a3c-85b7-7f54f35ea543" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Intervals on Which a Function Is Increasing Using Differentiation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c9041fc3-b3c2-513e-8267-87ec04aa78ff" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in (0,1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292293} {:answer/id #uuid "245ee93e-2db1-52bc-a177-afceb060f288" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in (0,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292294} {:answer/id #uuid "6ed137b1-88b7-57f9-b216-f1f7ede43161" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in (0,3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292295} {:answer/id #uuid "fe7be3f4-2c4e-5ae1-be46-3488948fffcf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in (3,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292296} {:answer/id #uuid "0e140852-b9dc-5e99-a4d3-4512ad29cead" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in (1,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292297}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0e140852-b9dc-5e99-a4d3-4512ad29cead" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in (1,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292297} :answer-field/id #uuid "96e80dd6-8f4d-5974-b059-92a5d5f4d3f8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292292}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "663fb6af-8507-5e62-bac7-090f5e3edc6e" :question/math-academy-id "q-21019" :question/problem "Given that $f(x)={x}^{3}+\\frac{3}{x}$ is defined for $x\\in (0,\\infty ),$ find the values of $x$ for which the function is strictly increasing." :question/worked-solution "The function is strictly increasing where ${f}^{′}(x)>0.$ Differentiating $f(x),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =3{x}^{2}-\\frac{3}{{x}^{2}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso we need to solve the inequality\n\n$$\n3{x}^{2}-\\frac{3}{{x}^{2}}>0.\n$$\n\nUsing that $x>0,$ the above inequality factors as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{x}^{2}-\\frac{3}{{x}^{2}} & >0 \\\\ {x}^{2}-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}} & >0 \\\\ {x}^{2}({x}^{2}-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}) & >0 \\\\ {x}^{4}-1 & >0 \\\\ ({x}^{2}+1)(x+1)(x-1) & >0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe factors ${x}^{2}+1$ and $x+1$ are positive, so this reduces to\n\n$$\nx-1>0.\n$$\n\nSolving this inequality, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x-1 & >0 \\\\ x & >1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe conclude that $f(x)$ is strictly increasing on $(1,\\infty ).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186292298 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3b466b35-cb4d-5082-9989-9c767af757f8" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Intervals on Which a Function Is Decreasing Using Differentiation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8dc9466a-ba33-599c-a377-df5c35fcb64a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292300}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8dc9466a-ba33-599c-a377-df5c35fcb64a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292300} :answer-field/id #uuid "06e6dc4d-d27b-576e-92a6-5d1574f68606" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292299} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8e75f2f3-ef44-594a-80ab-f93e4772707e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292302}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8e75f2f3-ef44-594a-80ab-f93e4772707e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292302} :answer-field/id #uuid "87d9b456-748b-5109-9440-4ede11ea60ab" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292301}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "1cf79dbe-8152-5ab4-8e50-e95e13f50f06" :question/math-academy-id "q-343536" :question/problem "Given that $g(x)=3{x}^{4}-8{x}^{3}+6{x}^{2}+1$ is defined for $x\\in (-8,8),$ find the values of $x$ for which the function is strictly decreasing.\n\n$x\\in (${{field-1}}$,$\n{{field-2}}$)$" :question/worked-solution "The function is strictly decreasing where ${g}^{′}(x)<0.$ Differentiating $g(x),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}^{′}(x) & =12{x}^{3}-24{x}^{2}+12x \\\\  & =12x({x}^{2}-2x+1) \\\\  & =12x{(x-1)}^{2},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso we need to solve the inequality\n\n$$\n12x{(x-1)}^{2}<0.\n$$\n\nSince $(x-1)^{2}\\ge 0$ for all real $x$, the sign of $12x{(x-1)}^{2}$ is determined by the sign of $12x$, so $12x{(x-1)}^{2}<0$ exactly when $12x<0.$ This yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}12x & <0 \\\\ x & <0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since the function is restricted to $x\\in (-8,8),$ we conclude that $g(x)$ is strictly decreasing on the interval $x\\in (-8,0).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186292303 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1671fb4f-937f-5a3c-85b7-7f54f35ea543" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Intervals on Which a Function Is Increasing Using Differentiation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1dc9d517-5024-5c53-b792-03b56e3d3695" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292305}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1dc9d517-5024-5c53-b792-03b56e3d3695" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292305} :answer-field/id #uuid "6e5e3246-f439-5276-879d-71aeb8663c62" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292304} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b8fb649d-4c24-507b-80b2-6474af2985ca" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292307}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b8fb649d-4c24-507b-80b2-6474af2985ca" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292307} :answer-field/id #uuid "064aa38e-49f2-545e-bf28-9c26cf65d539" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292306}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "80c35491-525d-5ce5-97f7-a307c606220b" :question/math-academy-id "q-344091" :question/problem "Find the values of $x$ for which the function $g(x)=\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{3}-\\frac{7}{4}{x}^{2}+\\frac{3}{2}x+1$ is strictly increasing.\n\n$x<$\n{{field-1}} $\\,$ and $\\,x>$\n{{field-2}}" :question/worked-solution "The function is strictly increasing where ${g}^{′}(x)>0.$ Differentiating $g(x),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}^{′}(x) & ={x}^{2}-\\frac{7}{2}x+\\frac{3}{2},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso we need to solve the inequality\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}-\\frac{7}{2}x+\\frac{3}{2}>0.\n$$\n\nThe above inequality factors as\n\n$$\n(x-\\frac{1}{2})(x-3)>0.\n$$\n\nThe graph of $y=(x-\\frac{1}{2})(x-3)$ has roots at $x=\\frac{1}{2}$ and $x=3,$ as shown below:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14014428/assets/26374f7e5c86c5585d384b3e65b4a1b3fb2f94172ed753cdb3fe7bdb0af0abb8.png)\n\nFor an upward-opening parabola, the product $(x-\\frac{1}{2})(x-3)$ is positive when $x<\\frac{1}{2}$ or $x>3.$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the function is strictly increasing on the intervals \n\n$$\nx<\\frac{1}{2}\\,\\text{and}\\,x>3.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186292308 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1671fb4f-937f-5a3c-85b7-7f54f35ea543" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Intervals on Which a Function Is Increasing Using Differentiation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7ad66654-01c5-567f-9727-029b99131e7f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292310} {:answer/id #uuid "7ee1c080-ab3b-5e52-8b67-b160b6d4aec6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292311}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7ee1c080-ab3b-5e52-8b67-b160b6d4aec6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292311} :answer-field/id #uuid "1d174429-194a-5a07-844a-aa0fe83880d1" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292309}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "0476db05-5de2-5374-aca7-f167f44d351f" :question/math-academy-id "q-344093" :question/problem "Find the values of $x$ for which the function $g(x)=\\frac{{x}^{4}}{4}-\\frac{4{x}^{3}}{3}$ is strictly increasing.\n\n$x>$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The function is strictly increasing where ${g}^{′}(x)>0.$ Differentiating $g(x),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}^{′}(x) & ={x}^{3}-4{x}^{2} \\\\  & ={x}^{2}(x-4),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso we need to solve the inequality\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}(x-4)>0.\n$$\n\nThe graph of $y={x}^{2}(x-4)$ has roots at $x=0$ and $x=4,$ as shown below:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14014428/assets/bfba80e21f4a458af6c4111191908a9d45d7d8c05f237db293cdad71a451d943.png)\n\nSince ${x}^{2}\\ge 0$ for all real $x,$ the product ${x}^{2}(x-4)$ is positive exactly when $x-4>0,$ that is, when $x>4.$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the function is strictly increasing on the interval \n\n$$\nx>4.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186292312 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1671fb4f-937f-5a3c-85b7-7f54f35ea543" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Intervals on Which a Function Is Increasing Using Differentiation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "36fd1802-36cc-5158-95f1-7e974dd547e1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\pi <x<0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292314} {:answer/id #uuid "9cbf9759-de6f-55a0-86ef-a0eac25ea64f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\pi }{4}<x<0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292315} {:answer/id #uuid "d01aaa29-954c-534d-b66f-4d49291ad017" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\pi }{2}<x<0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292316} {:answer/id #uuid "92347dca-c256-5bae-99ae-abb6e552cc48" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\pi <x<-\\frac{\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292317} {:answer/id #uuid "562cd83c-5e19-521a-99d6-0a274ac06495" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2\\pi <x<\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292318}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "36fd1802-36cc-5158-95f1-7e974dd547e1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\pi <x<0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292314} :answer-field/id #uuid "60da7cc2-e305-5411-88c4-64a71faf4cd7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292313}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "4bb806c1-d058-5c6e-9c3d-eb04de3f0030" :question/math-academy-id "q-48855" :question/problem "Given that $f(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{x}{2})$ is defined for $x\\in (-2\\pi ,0),$ find the values of $x$ for which the function is strictly increasing." :question/worked-solution "The function is strictly increasing where ${f}^{′}(x)>0.$ Differentiating $f(x),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x)=\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{x}{2}),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso we need to solve the inequality\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{x}{2})>0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the domain is $x\\in (-2\\pi ,0),$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2\\pi  & <x<0 \\\\ -\\pi  & <\\frac{x}{2}<0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe know that cosine is positive in the first and the fourth quadrant. So, this reduces to\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{\\pi }{2} & <\\frac{x}{2}<0 \\\\ -\\pi  & <x<0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $f(x)$ is strictly increasing on the interval $-\\pi <x<0.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186292319 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1671fb4f-937f-5a3c-85b7-7f54f35ea543" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Intervals on Which a Function Is Increasing Using Differentiation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fc711fe5-b65c-5a22-909f-1be8c43ad06a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in (\\frac{1}{e},e)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292321} {:answer/id #uuid "6e8e44c1-c248-5e64-9411-5bf5220c5424" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in (0,e)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292322} {:answer/id #uuid "432dc720-9e17-5ba8-9478-6ad706849a04" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in (e,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292323} {:answer/id #uuid "6b253c72-0412-543f-aaf9-c3c2acb9f20a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in (0,\\frac{1}{e})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292324} {:answer/id #uuid "6fa7ebbf-a86c-5ebf-b55c-794fc32e3ab3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in (\\frac{1}{e},\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292325}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6fa7ebbf-a86c-5ebf-b55c-794fc32e3ab3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in (\\frac{1}{e},\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292325} :answer-field/id #uuid "74ee2adc-bfa3-56a0-be8c-af463760c17a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292320}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "680534fd-cbc6-5a80-9a00-31c6d6540a49" :question/math-academy-id "q-48860" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14014428/assets/61b89de34a6900d2ea39e5c2af141b3353e9ca790ac2748d0fc6d4d578127f93.png)\n\nFind the values of $x$ for which the function $f(x)=x\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$ (plotted above) is strictly increasing." :question/worked-solution "The function is strictly increasing where ${f}^{′}(x)>0.$ Differentiating $f(x)$ using the product  rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =1\\cdot \\operatorname{ln}⁡x+\\frac{1}{x}\\cdot x \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+1,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso we need to solve the inequality\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+1>0.\n$$\n\nSolving $\\operatorname{ln}⁡x=-1$ gives the critical point\n\n$$\nx={e}^{-1}\\,⟹\\,x=\\frac{1}{e}.\n$$\n\nFrom the graph, we see that this critical point is a minimum. We also see that the slope of the tangent lines is negative to the left of the critical point and positive to the right of the critical point. \n\nSo, we summarize the sign of the derivative in the following table:\n\n$$\n\\left.\\begin{aligned}x & (0,\\frac{1}{e}) & \\frac{1}{e} & (\\frac{1}{e},\\infty ) \\\\ f & ↘ & \\text{min} & ↗ \\\\ {f}^{′} & - & 0 & +\\end{aligned}\\right|\n$$\n\nFinally, we conclude that the function is strictly increasing on the interval $(\\frac{1}{e},\\infty ).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106288 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b77217ef-9076-5365-bebb-d2b46e64c9be" :knowledge-point/title "Comparing Slopes of Functions at Different Points"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8c3b1fb7-c5f4-5584-bcdd-0fadb99fe83e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f^{′}(0) < f^{′}(1) < f^{′}(3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186104238} {:answer/id #uuid "ccda6cd1-fb01-5eaa-ad36-c61e86021991" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f^{′}(1) < f^{′}(0) < f^{′}(3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186104239} {:answer/id #uuid "29798f0a-acb2-5f3e-8c58-0507c31b6983" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f^{′}(0) < f^{′}(3) < f^{′}(1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186104240} {:answer/id #uuid "36710fe9-febe-5e14-82be-721b4fd6e2b1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f^{′}(3) < f^{′}(0) < f^{′}(1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186104241} {:answer/id #uuid "6033e3ff-be38-59f8-98c9-f455bb8ab000" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f^{′}(1) < f^{′}(3) < f^{′}(0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186104242}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6033e3ff-be38-59f8-98c9-f455bb8ab000" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f^{′}(1) < f^{′}(3) < f^{′}(0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186104242} :answer-field/id #uuid "cf6956d2-b211-58bd-b5cb-d455beaf513a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186104237}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f4beddd7-ca54-5c40-8a6b-2e4b3e0488da" :question/math-academy-id "q-48996" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14014428/assets/0a55a722c0b1f22b7a3d4c1f8d69869aa6a7dd571f85349049389c105b7190aa.png)\n\nConsider the graph of $y=f(x)$ above. Which of the following statements is true?" :question/worked-solution "From the graph, we see that\n\n- The slope of the tangent at $x=0$ is positive, so ${f}^{′}(0)>0.$\n\n- The slope of the tangent at $x=1$ is negative, so ${f}^{′}(1)<0.$\n\n- The slope of the tangent at $x=3$ is zero, so ${f}^{′}(3)=0.$\n\nTherefore, ${f}^{′}(1)<{f}^{′}(3)<{f}^{′}(0).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186292326 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b77217ef-9076-5365-bebb-d2b46e64c9be" :knowledge-point/title "Comparing Slopes of Functions at Different Points"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0c6172c5-087b-518a-bff2-2ae51e8d4ec9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}^{′}(1)<{f}^{′}(-2)<{f}^{′}(-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292328} {:answer/id #uuid "5287580a-9338-5293-b8cb-9a9446a9eca6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}^{′}(1)<{f}^{′}(-1)<{f}^{′}(-2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292329} {:answer/id #uuid "64f7ceca-6673-54dc-a9a6-800ca55235a1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}^{′}(-2)<{f}^{′}(1)<{f}^{′}(-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292330} {:answer/id #uuid "dc16cb13-d6fd-53eb-9556-6c5b2d400168" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}^{′}(-2)<{f}^{′}(-1)<{f}^{′}(1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292331} {:answer/id #uuid "938e2683-9878-5c90-8a72-51090e228074" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}^{′}(-1)<{f}^{′}(1)<{f}^{′}(-2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292332}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "64f7ceca-6673-54dc-a9a6-800ca55235a1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}^{′}(-2)<{f}^{′}(1)<{f}^{′}(-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292330} :answer-field/id #uuid "ccd41fc8-90f1-5385-b65a-e2db6923c18a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292327}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e31c47ec-52ee-5c63-a42a-61e28cfe1717" :question/math-academy-id "q-49006" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14014428/assets/ae52c0cfa5238c68574fdc3eff16618b84ce2dfaa5846df5300bd5602b8628f5.png)\n\nGiven the graph of $y=f(x)$ above, which of the following statements is true?" :question/worked-solution "From the graph, we see that\n\n- The slope of the tangent at $x=-2$ is negative, so ${f}^{′}(-2)<0.$\n\n- The slope of the tangent at $x=-1$ is positive, so ${f}^{′}(-1)>0.$\n\n- The slope of the tangent at $x=1$ is zero, so ${f}^{′}(1)=0.$\n\nTherefore , ${f}^{′}(-2)<{f}^{′}(1)<{f}^{′}(-1).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186292333 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a87f2499-6998-587a-930e-65845c94710c" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Intervals on Which the Derivative of a Graphed Function Is Positive or Negative"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d2a47729-94cf-5841-ba84-97f35f478abb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(3,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292335} {:answer/id #uuid "ad907457-ad97-56db-b096-0bcf5527a020" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(-\\infty ,0)$ and  $(3,\\infty )$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292336} {:answer/id #uuid "632a4af9-3825-5d50-aefc-d73d849e94b1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292337} {:answer/id #uuid "f1fafb40-8ed9-5087-bb22-42d7590eea58" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "No interval exists where ${f}^{′}(x)>0$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292338} {:answer/id #uuid "18bc1fab-c333-5e97-ba8a-fda1f0991d94" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-\\infty ,0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292339}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "632a4af9-3825-5d50-aefc-d73d849e94b1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292337} :answer-field/id #uuid "c52a959b-3c9f-5fe3-be77-12a346c86ff7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292334}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "661e6b85-8613-5f9a-9927-c76d05b988ae" :question/math-academy-id "q-73443" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14014428/assets/e0adac4b5e6a66f6c6a38af96f2e55c05ec98a87857af736e40d22b31fdf259b.png)\n\nThe graph of the continuous function $y=f(x)$ is shown above. The points $A$ and $B$ lie on the curve and have $x$-coordinates $x=0$ and $x=3.$ Given that ${f}^{′}(x)=0$ at both $A$ and $B,$ find the intervals where ${f}^{′}(x)>0.$" :question/worked-solution "We have ${f}^{′}(x)=0$ at the critical points $A$ and $B.$ Note the following:\n\n- If $f(x)$ is strictly increasing, then ${f}^{′}(x)>0.$\n\n- If $f(x)$ is strictly decreasing, then ${f}^{′}(x)<0.$\n\nThe information about ${f}^{′}(x)$ can be summarized in the following table:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & (-\\infty ,0) & 0 & (0,3) & 3 & (3,\\infty ) \\\\ f & ↘ & \\text{min} & ↗ & \\text{max} & ↘ \\\\ {f}^{′} & - & 0 & + & 0 & -\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${f}^{′}(x)>0$ on the interval $(0,3).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186292340 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b77217ef-9076-5365-bebb-d2b46e64c9be" :knowledge-point/title "Comparing Slopes of Functions at Different Points"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:an